x.explanation+='\n\nThe code exposes both loops with a half-open range `[lo, hi)` and `hi = len(nums)`. The lower loop moves right only for `< target`; the upper loop moves right for `<= target`. `lo` is an insertion point, not proof of presence: check `i < len(nums) and nums[i] == target` before treating the lower bound as a match. `bisect.insort` first searches in O(log n), then inserts/shifts in O(n).';
x.vocabulary.find(v=>v.term==='Lower bound').definition='First index with value >= target, or len(nums) if no value qualifies.';x.vocabulary.find(v=>v.term==='Upper bound').definition='First index with value > target, or len(nums) if no value qualifies.';
x.concepts.tradeoffs+=' insort is O(n) because inserting into a list may shift the tail; it is not an O(log n) update.';
x.complexity.push({operation:'bisect.insort',worst:'O(n)',space:'O(1)',note:'Amortized insertion storage beyond the maintained list; a resize can copy O(n) references. Search is O(log n), shifting/insertion dominates.'});
x.complexityExplanation.derivation=[{lines:[6,7,8,9,11],description:'Lower-bound iterations halve the candidate index range.',cost:'O(log n)',dimension:'time'},{lines:[16,17,18,19,21],description:'Upper-bound iterations halve the range; equality moves right.',cost:'O(log n)',dimension:'time'},{lines:[5,15],description:'Each query uses only scalar indices.',cost:'O(1)',dimension:'space'}];x.complexityExplanation.counters=[{label:'boundary midpoint tests',definition:'executions of a lower or upper midpoint comparison',countLines:[8,18]}];x.bindings=[{variable:'nums',model:'array',overlays:[{role:'boundary',label:'lo',source:'lo'},{role:'pointer',label:'mid',source:'mid'},{role:'boundary',label:'hi (exclusive)',source:'hi'}]}];
